343,subspecies,Anseriformes,Anatidae,"Ducks, Swans, and Geese",Anser albifrons sponsa,"Banks, RC, 2011",Proceedings of the Biological Society of Washington 124 p.231,,,,,,breeds in western Alaska; winters in California and western Mexico,,,,gwfgoo6,,avibase-24FB6A25,,,,Taxonomy of Greater White-fronted Geese,http://www.jeaniron.ca/2011/Banks-GWFG-PBSW-11.pdf,Anser albifrons sponsa
344,species,Anseriformes,Anatidae,"Ducks, Swans, and Geese",Anser serrirostris,"Gould, J, 1852",The Literary Gazette no.1836 p.306,Tundra Bean Goose,Tundra Bean-Goose,,8,"Taxon serrirostris (polytypic, including rossicus) is treated as a species separate from Anser fabalis (polytypic, including johanseni and middendorffii) based on available evidence. The species are morphologically diagnosable (de Jong 2019) and ecologically separated to a large extent (Sangster & Oreel 1996). Genetic data are complicated by a history of introgressive hybridization (Ottenburghs et al. 2020, 2023), rapid speciation (Ottenburghs et al. 2016), and incongruent phylogenetic reconstructions (Ruokenen et al. 2008; Ottenburghs et al. 2016, 2020, 2023). However, genomic data consistently show A. brachyrhynchus to be nested within the fabalis-serrirostris complex (Ottenburghs et al. 2016, 2023) with highly differentiated genomic regions providing strong support for a 3-species treatment (Ottenburghs et al. 2023): A. fabalis, A. brachyrhynchus, and A. serrirostris. For taxonomic stability, middendorffii is treated as a subspecies of A. fabalis, but a more comprehensive genomic review is needed to determine if species status is warranted (Ruokenen et al. 2008).",,,NE,,tunbeg1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/tunbeg1/,avibase-AD849FCB,,,"Shanghai, China.",,https://archive.org/details/sim_literary-gazette_1852-03-27_1836/page/306/mode/2up,Anser serrirostris
345,subspecies,Anseriformes,Anatidae,"Ducks, Swans, and Geese",Anser serrirostris rossicus,"Buturlin, SA, 1933",Opredelitel promyslovykh ptits p.60,,,,,,breeds tundra of northern Russia and northwestern Siberia eastwards to the Taimyr Peninsula; winters mainly in western and central Europe and southwestern Asia,,,,tunbeg2,,avibase-0260E792,,,"western Siberia = Beluchia Guba, Samal, Taymyr Peninsula, fide Dementiev, 1936, Alauda, 8, p. 190.",,,Anser (Melanonyx) serrirostris rossicus
