3625,species,Gruiformes,Rallidae,"Rails, Gallinules, and Coots",Rufirallus fasciatus,"(Sclater, PL; Salvin, O, 1868)","Proceedings of the Zoological Society of London 1867, pt.3 p.981",Black-banded Crake,Black-banded Crake,Black-banded Crake,1159,"An interim decision on species limits in Laterallus is based mostly on the multilocus, nuclear, and genomic data of Stervander et al. (2019), Garcia-R et al. (2020), Kirchman et al. (2021) and Depino et al. (2023), supported in part by vocal, morphological and ecological evidence. Accordingly, a polytypic Rufirallus is recognized, with fasciatus, leucopyrrhus, and xenopterus placed there, along with viridis and schomburgkii, as these form a monophyletic clade (Depino et al. 2023) sister to the remaining genera; taxon castaneiceps has been placed in Rufirallus, but placement in a monotypic Anurolimnas is well supported by recent studies (Kirchman et al. 2021; Depino et al. 2023); although genetic data are lacking, taxon podarces is placed in the monotypic genus Aphanocrex based on its distinctive morphology; taxon notatus is placed in Laterallus rather than Coturnicops based on Depino et al. (2023); all remaining species (melanophaius, levraudi, albigularis, ruber, exilis, rogersi, spiloptera, spilonota, jamaicensis and flaviventer) are placed in Laterallus. Further revisions are likely subject to publication of ongoing integrative research.",marshes of southeastern Colombia to eastern Peru and western Amazonian Brazil,,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22692330,blbcra1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/blbcra1/,avibase-BE9214E6,,,,"List of Birds collected at Pebas, Upper Amazons, by Mr. John Hauxwell, with Notes and Descriptions of New Species.",https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/90421#page/415/mode/1up,Porzana fasciata
3626,species,Gruiformes,Rallidae,"Rails, Gallinules, and Coots",Rufirallus leucopyrrhus,"(Vieillot, LJP, 1819)","Nouveau dictionnaire d'histoire naturelle, appliquée aux arts, à l'agriculture, à l'économie rurale et domestique, à la médecine, etc. Nouvelle édition 28 p.550",Red-and-white Crake,Red-and-white Crake,Red-and-white Crake,1159,"An interim decision on species limits in Laterallus is based mostly on the multilocus, nuclear, and genomic data of Stervander et al. (2019), Garcia-R et al. (2020), Kirchman et al. (2021) and Depino et al. (2023), supported in part by vocal, morphological and ecological evidence. Accordingly, a polytypic Rufirallus is recognized, with fasciatus, leucopyrrhus, and xenopterus placed there, along with viridis and schomburgkii, as these form a monophyletic clade (Depino et al. 2023) sister to the remaining genera; taxon castaneiceps has been placed in Rufirallus, but placement in a monotypic Anurolimnas is well supported by recent studies (Kirchman et al. 2021; Depino et al. 2023); although genetic data are lacking, taxon podarces is placed in the monotypic genus Aphanocrex based on its distinctive morphology; taxon notatus is placed in Laterallus rather than Coturnicops based on Depino et al. (2023); all remaining species (melanophaius, levraudi, albigularis, ruber, exilis, rogersi, spiloptera, spilonota, jamaicensis and flaviventer) are placed in Laterallus. Further revisions are likely subject to publication of ongoing integrative research.","marshes of southeastern Brazil to Uruguay, Paraguay, and northern Argentina",,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22692371,rawcra1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/rawcra1/,avibase-68948FC6,,,"Paraguay, ex Azara.",,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/63936#page/580/mode/1up,Rallus leucopyrrhus
3627,species,Gruiformes,Rallidae,"Rails, Gallinules, and Coots",Rufirallus xenopterus,"(Conover, HB, 1934)","The Auk 51, no.3 p.365",Rufous-faced Crake,Rufous-faced Crake,Rufous-faced Crake,1159,"An interim decision on species limits in Laterallus is based mostly on the multilocus, nuclear, and genomic data of Stervander et al. (2019), Garcia-R et al. (2020), Kirchman et al. (2021) and Depino et al. (2023), supported in part by vocal, morphological and ecological evidence. Accordingly, a polytypic Rufirallus is recognized, with fasciatus, leucopyrrhus, and xenopterus placed there, along with viridis and schomburgkii, as these form a monophyletic clade (Depino et al. 2023) sister to the remaining genera; taxon castaneiceps has been placed in Rufirallus, but placement in a monotypic Anurolimnas is well supported by recent studies (Kirchman et al. 2021; Depino et al. 2023); although genetic data are lacking, taxon podarces is placed in the monotypic genus Aphanocrex based on its distinctive morphology; taxon notatus is placed in Laterallus rather than Coturnicops based on Depino et al. (2023); all remaining species (melanophaius, levraudi, albigularis, ruber, exilis, rogersi, spiloptera, spilonota, jamaicensis and flaviventer) are placed in Laterallus. Further revisions are likely subject to publication of ongoing integrative research.","marshes of southeastern Paraguay, Bolivia (Beni), and adjacent southeastern Brazil",,VU,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22692374,rufcra1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/rufcra1/,avibase-99374A39,,,,A new species of Rail from Paraguay.,https://sora.unm.edu/sites/default/files/journals/auk/v051n03/p0365-p0366.pdf,Laterallus xenopterus
