3632,subspecies,Gruiformes,Rallidae,"Rails, Gallinules, and Coots",Coturnicops noveboracensis goldmani,"(Nelson, EW, 1904)",Proceedings of the Biological Society of Washington 17 p.151,,,,,,known only from marshes near Río Lerma (central Mexico); extinct (last reported in the early 1960s),Extinct,,,yelrai2,,avibase-86546A68,,,"Lerma, Mexico.",Descriptions of four new birds from Mexico.,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/22854#page/169/mode/1up,Porzana goldmani
3633,genus,Gruiformes,Rallidae,"Rails, Gallinules, and Coots",Laterallus,"Gray, GR, 1855",Catalogue of the genera and subgenera of birds contained in the British Museum p.120,,,,1159,"An interim decision on species limits in Laterallus is based mostly on the multilocus, nuclear, and genomic data of Stervander et al. (2019), Garcia-R et al. (2020), Kirchman et al. (2021) and Depino et al. (2023), supported in part by vocal, morphological and ecological evidence. Accordingly, a polytypic Rufirallus is recognized, with fasciatus, leucopyrrhus, and xenopterus placed there, along with viridis and schomburgkii, as these form a monophyletic clade (Depino et al. 2023) sister to the remaining genera; taxon castaneiceps has been placed in Rufirallus, but placement in a monotypic Anurolimnas is well supported by recent studies (Kirchman et al. 2021; Depino et al. 2023); although genetic data are lacking, taxon podarces is placed in the monotypic genus Aphanocrex based on its distinctive morphology; taxon notatus is placed in Laterallus rather than Coturnicops based on Depino et al. (2023); all remaining species (melanophaius, levraudi, albigularis, ruber, exilis, rogersi, spiloptera, spilonota, jamaicensis and flaviventer) are placed in Laterallus. Further revisions are likely subject to publication of ongoing integrative research.",,,,,,,,M,"Rallus melanophaius [Vieillot, 1819]; type by original designation",,,,Laterallus
