3823,genus,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Hoploxypterus,"Bonaparte, CLJL, 1856","Comptes Rendus Hebdomadaires des Séances de l'Académie des Sciences 43, no.8 p.418",,,,907,"Taxon cayanus is placed in the monospecific genus Hoploxypterus based on differences in morphology, osteology, and behavior that place it outside Vanellus (Fjeldså & Nielsen 1989; Livezey 2010). This treatment is supported by limited mitochondrial DNA data (Černý & Natale 2022), although the exact placement of H. cayanus remains unresolved.",,,,,,,,M,Charadrius cayanus [Latham]; type by monotypy,,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/16553#page/430/mode/1up,Hoploxypterus
3824,species,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Hoploxypterus cayanus,"(Latham, J, 1790)","Index ornithologicus, sive systema ornithologiæ; complectens avium divisionem in classes, ordines, genera, species, ipsarumque varietates: adjectis synonymis, locis, descriptionibus, &c. 2 p.749",Pied Plover,Pied Plover,Pied Plover,907,"Taxon cayanus is placed in the monospecific genus Hoploxypterus based on differences in morphology, osteology, and behavior that place it outside Vanellus (Fjeldså & Nielsen 1989; Livezey 2010). This treatment is supported by limited mitochondrial DNA data (Černý & Natale 2022), although the exact placement of H. cayanus remains unresolved.","riverbanks and mudflats of lowlands to hills in tropical South America east of the Andes south to Paraguay, south-eastern Brazil, and (rarely) north-eastern Argentina; west of the Andes only in western Ecuador",,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22694072,pielap1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/pielap1/,avibase-FC943B05,,,Cayenne.,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/226469#page/289/mode/1up,Charadrius cayanus
3825,genus,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Phegornis,"Gray, GR, 1846","The genera of birds: comprising their generic characters, a notice of the habits of each genus, and an extensive list of species referred to their several genera 3, pt.35 p.545",,,,,,,,,,,,,M,"Leptopus mitchellii [Fraser, 1845]; type by original designation",,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/148103#page/158/mode/1up,Phegornis
3826,species,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Phegornis mitchellii,"(Fraser, L, 1845)","Proceedings of the Zoological Society of London pt.12, no.140 p.157",Diademed Sandpiper-Plover,Diademed Sandpiper-Plover,Diademed Plover,,,high-elevation bogs of Andes from central Peru to central Chile and western Argentina,,NT,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22693931,diaplo1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/diaplo1/,avibase-0BFE075B,,,"Chile, probably in the Province of Colchagua.",Exhibition of species of Chilian Birds with description of a new bird from Chile.,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/46217#page/169/mode/1up,Leptopus (Leptodactylus) Mitchellii
3827,genus,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Zonibyx,"Reichenbach, HGL, 1853",Avium Systema Naturale. Das natürliche System der Vögel mit hundert Tafeln grösstentheils Original-Abbildungen der bis jetzt entdecken fast zwölfhundert typischen Formen. Vorlaüfer einer Iconographie der Arten der Vögel aller Welttheile p.xviii.,,,,1075,"Based mostly on the phylogenetic analyses of Dos Remedios et al. (2015) and Černý & Natale (2022), the expanded genus Charadrius is split into multiple genera: a monotypic Eudromias (E. morinellus); a reduced Charadrius (polytypic; C. vociferus, C. hiaticula, C. semipalmatus, and C. melodus); a monotypic Zonibyx (Z. modestus); a polytypic Thinornis (including T. cucullatus, T. melanops, T. novaeseelandiae, T. forbesi, T. tricollaris, T. dubius, and T. placidus); five monotypic genera not included in the phylogeny by Dos Remedios et al. (2015) (Phegornis, Oreopholus, Elseyornis, Erythrogonys, and Peltohyas); and a large polytypic Anarhynchus comprising the remaining species previously placed in Charadrius. Further research to refine generic limits in the Charadriidae will be needed.",,,,,,,,F,Vanellus cinctus [Lesson]; type by monotypy,,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/103886#page/86/mode/1up,Zonibyx
3828,species,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Zonibyx modestus,"(Lichtenstein, MHC, 1823)","Verzeichniss der Doubletten des zoologischen Museums der Königl. Universität zu Berlin nebst Beschreibung vieler bisher unbekannter Arten von Säugethieren, Vögeln, Amphibien und Fischen p.71",Rufous-chested Dotterel,Rufous-chested Dotterel,Rufous-chested Plover,,,"breeds inland south-central Chile, southern Argentina, and Falkland Islands; winters to coastal southern Peru and southeastern Brazil",,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22693879,rucdot1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/rucdot1/,avibase-42D62A12,,,"Montevideo, Uruguay.",,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/89675#page/91/mode/1up,Charadrius modestus
3829,genus,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Eudromias,"Brehm, CL, 1830",Isis von Oken 23 col.987,,,,1075,"Based mostly on the phylogenetic analyses of Dos Remedios et al. (2015) and Černý & Natale (2022), the expanded genus Charadrius is split into multiple genera: a monotypic Eudromias (E. morinellus); a reduced Charadrius (polytypic; C. vociferus, C. hiaticula, C. semipalmatus, and C. melodus); a monotypic Zonibyx (Z. modestus); a polytypic Thinornis (including T. cucullatus, T. melanops, T. novaeseelandiae, T. forbesi, T. tricollaris, T. dubius, and T. placidus); five monotypic genera not included in the phylogeny by Dos Remedios et al. (2015) (Phegornis, Oreopholus, Elseyornis, Erythrogonys, and Peltohyas); and a large polytypic Anarhynchus comprising the remaining species previously placed in Charadrius. Further research to refine generic limits in the Charadriidae will be needed.",,,,,,,,M,"Eudromias morinella [Boie]; type by monotypy = Charadrius morinellus Linnaeus, 1758",,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/87982#page/524/mode/1up,Eudromias
3830,species,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Eudromias morinellus,"(Linnaeus, C, 1758)","Systema Naturæ per Regna Tria Naturæ, Secundum Classes, Ordines, Genera, Species, cum Characteribus, Differentiis, Synonymis, Locis. Tomus I. Editio decima, reformata 1 p.150",Eurasian Dotterel,Eurasian Dotterel,Eurasian Dotterel,,,"breeds high Arctic coastal tundra and alpine zone, from northern British Isles and Scandinavia eastward to Chukotskiy Peninsula and western Alaska; in south locally from Pyrenees through montane Mongolia; winters from Iberian Peninsula and northern Africa eastward to Arabian Peninsula and Iraq",,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22693906,eurdot,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/eurdot/,avibase-20BE9DF1,,,"Europe, restricted type locality Sweden, apud Hartert, p. 1545.",,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/10277#page/169/mode/1up,Charadrius Morinellus
3831,genus,Charadriiformes,Charadriidae,Plovers and Lapwings,Charadrius,"Linnaeus, C, 1758","Systema Naturæ per Regna Tria Naturæ, Secundum Classes, Ordines, Genera, Species, cum Characteribus, Differentiis, Synonymis, Locis. Tomus I. Editio decima, reformata 1 p.150",,,,1075,"Based mostly on the phylogenetic analyses of Dos Remedios et al. (2015) and Černý & Natale (2022), the expanded genus Charadrius is split into multiple genera: a monotypic Eudromias (E. morinellus); a reduced Charadrius (polytypic; C. vociferus, C. hiaticula, C. semipalmatus, and C. melodus); a monotypic Zonibyx (Z. modestus); a polytypic Thinornis (including T. cucullatus, T. melanops, T. novaeseelandiae, T. forbesi, T. tricollaris, T. dubius, and T. placidus); five monotypic genera not included in the phylogeny by Dos Remedios et al. (2015) (Phegornis, Oreopholus, Elseyornis, Erythrogonys, and Peltohyas); and a large polytypic Anarhynchus comprising the remaining species previously placed in Charadrius. Further research to refine generic limits in the Charadriidae will be needed.",,,,,,,,M,"Charadrius hiaticula [Linnaeus, 1758]; type by Linnaean tautonymy",,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/10277#page/169/mode/1up,Charadrius
