4608,species,Charadriiformes,Laridae,"Skimmers, Noddies, Terns, and Gulls",Larus smithsonianus,"Coues, E, 1862",Proceedings of the Academy of Natural Sciences of Philadelphia 14 p.296,American Herring Gull,American Herring Gull,,1136,"Four species are recognized in the Larus argentatus complex based on a combination of genetics, plumage, and vocalizations: polytypic L. argentatus (including argenteus); monotypic L. smithsonianus; monotypic L. vegae; and monotypic L. mongolicus. Mitochondrial and nuclear DNA data consistently support separation of L. smithsonianus from L. argentatus (Liebers et al. 2004; Černý & Natale 2022; Sternkopf et al. 2010; Sonsthagen et al. 2016), an arrangement also supported by differences in adult and juvenile plumage, and apparently vocalizations. Species L. vegae is sometimes considered a subspecies of L. smithsonianus but differs phenotypically at a level more consistent with species status in this complex. Finally, L. mongolicus was previously placed in L. cachinnans but is phenotypically distinct from that species and from L. vegae with which it is sometimes aligned; its vocalizations also appear to be the most distinctive of the species formerly placed in L. argentatus. A comprehensive genomic DNA analysis would be informative given the complex demographic and biogeographic history of this group.","breeds northern and western Alaska eastward across subarctic Canada to Baffin Island and Newfoundland, southward on Pacific coast to southern British Columbia, eastward to inland northeastern USA and Atlantic coast southward to North Carolina; winters from Aleutian Islands southward on Pacific coast to Nayarit (western Mexico), Gulf coast, and inland to Honduras and northern Caribbean",,NE,,amhgul1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/amhgul1/,avibase-60F32AF8,,,Eastern and western coasts of North America.,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/18281#page/308/mode/1up,Larus Smithsonianus
4609,species,Charadriiformes,Laridae,"Skimmers, Noddies, Terns, and Gulls",Larus vegae,"Palmén, JA, 1887",Vega-expeditionens vetenskapliga iakttagelser bearbetade af deltagare i resan och andra forskare 5 p.370,Vega Gull,Vega Gull,,1136,"Four species are recognized in the Larus argentatus complex based on a combination of genetics, plumage, and vocalizations: polytypic L. argentatus (including argenteus); monotypic L. smithsonianus; monotypic L. vegae; and monotypic L. mongolicus. Mitochondrial and nuclear DNA data consistently support separation of L. smithsonianus from L. argentatus (Liebers et al. 2004; Černý & Natale 2022; Sternkopf et al. 2010; Sonsthagen et al. 2016), an arrangement also supported by differences in adult and juvenile plumage, and apparently vocalizations. Species L. vegae is sometimes considered a subspecies of L. smithsonianus but differs phenotypically at a level more consistent with species status in this complex. Finally, L. mongolicus was previously placed in L. cachinnans but is phenotypically distinct from that species and from L. vegae with which it is sometimes aligned; its vocalizations also appear to be the most distinctive of the species formerly placed in L. argentatus. A comprehensive genomic DNA analysis would be informative given the complex demographic and biogeographic history of this group.","breeds Arctic central and eastern Siberia from Taymyr eastward to Chukotka, New Siberian Islands, and St. Lawrence Island (Bering Sea); winters mostly to rocky coasts of Japan, Korean Peninsula, and eastern China",,NE,,veggul1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/veggul1/,avibase-FD1EF7BB,,,"Pidlin, northeastern Siberia.",,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/133380#page/392/mode/1up,Larus argentatus Brünn. var. Vegae
4610,species,Charadriiformes,Laridae,"Skimmers, Noddies, Terns, and Gulls",Larus mongolicus,"Sushkin, PP, 1925",Spisok i raspredelenie ptits Russkogo Altaia i blizhaishikh chastei Severo-Zapadnoi Mongolii: s opisaniem novykh ili maloizvestnykh form p.63,Mongolian Gull,Mongolian Gull,,1136,"Four species are recognized in the Larus argentatus complex based on a combination of genetics, plumage, and vocalizations: polytypic L. argentatus (including argenteus); monotypic L. smithsonianus; monotypic L. vegae; and monotypic L. mongolicus. Mitochondrial and nuclear DNA data consistently support separation of L. smithsonianus from L. argentatus (Liebers et al. 2004; Černý & Natale 2022; Sternkopf et al. 2010; Sonsthagen et al. 2016), an arrangement also supported by differences in adult and juvenile plumage, and apparently vocalizations. Species L. vegae is sometimes considered a subspecies of L. smithsonianus but differs phenotypically at a level more consistent with species status in this complex. Finally, L. mongolicus was previously placed in L. cachinnans but is phenotypically distinct from that species and from L. vegae with which it is sometimes aligned; its vocalizations also appear to be the most distinctive of the species formerly placed in L. argentatus. A comprehensive genomic DNA analysis would be informative given the complex demographic and biogeographic history of this group.","breeds Altai Republic to Lake Baikal (south-central Russia) and Mongolia, Lake Khanka (Heilongjiang, northeastern China), and Yellow Sea islands (western Korean Peninsula); winters mostly to coastal wetlands of Japan, Korean Peninsula, eastern China, and Taiwan",,NE,,casgul4,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/casgul4/,avibase-E1325FAB,,,"Uriug-nor, northwestern Mongolia.",,,Larus argentatus mongolicus
4611,species,Charadriiformes,Laridae,"Skimmers, Noddies, Terns, and Gulls",Larus argentatus,"Pontoppidan, E, 1763",Den Danske Atlas eller Konge-Riget Dannemark (etc.) 1 p.622,European Herring Gull,European Herring Gull,European Herring Gull,1136,"Four species are recognized in the Larus argentatus complex based on a combination of genetics, plumage, and vocalizations: polytypic L. argentatus (including argenteus); monotypic L. smithsonianus; monotypic L. vegae; and monotypic L. mongolicus. Mitochondrial and nuclear DNA data consistently support separation of L. smithsonianus from L. argentatus (Liebers et al. 2004; Černý & Natale 2022; Sternkopf et al. 2010; Sonsthagen et al. 2016), an arrangement also supported by differences in adult and juvenile plumage, and apparently vocalizations. Species L. vegae is sometimes considered a subspecies of L. smithsonianus but differs phenotypically at a level more consistent with species status in this complex. Finally, L. mongolicus was previously placed in L. cachinnans but is phenotypically distinct from that species and from L. vegae with which it is sometimes aligned; its vocalizations also appear to be the most distinctive of the species formerly placed in L. argentatus. A comprehensive genomic DNA analysis would be informative given the complex demographic and biogeographic history of this group.",,,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/62030608,euhgul1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/euhgul1/,avibase-F002188E,,,"No type locality' = Christiansoe, ex Brunnich, Orn. Boreal., 1764, p. 44.",,,Larus Argentatus
