14188,genus,Passeriformes,Tyrannidae,Tyrant Flycatchers and Allies,Syrtidicola,"Chesser, RT; Harvey, MG; Brumfield, RT; Derryberry, EP, 2020","Proceedings of the Biological Society of Washington 133, no.1 p.35-48",,,,471,"Genus-level changes in the fluvicoline tyrant flycatchers are based on a combination of genetic (summarised by Chesser et al. 2020b; Ohlson et al. 2020), phenotypic, and behavioral data. The classification recognizes: Syrtidicola fluviatilis based on deep genetic divergence from Muscisaxicola (Chesser et al. 2020b), as well as morphological and ecological differences; Cnemarchus rufipennis based on common themes in plumage, vocalizations and ecology with C. erythropygius; Pyrope pyrope based on deep genetic divergence within the Xolmis sensu lato clade (Chesser et al. 2020b; Ohlson et al. 2020); monospecific Nengetus (N. cinereus) based on deep genetic divergence, and its distinctive plumage and ecology; Neoxolmis restricted to four species (N. rufiventris, N. rubetra, N. salinarum, and N. coronatus) which form a cohesive group based on plumage and ecology to the exclusion of cinereus; Xolmis restricted to X. irupero and X. velatus based on genetics; and Heteroxolmis dominicanus, as genetic data show it to be outside the Xolmiini and sister to Alectrurus (Chesser et al. 2020b; Ohlson et al. 2020) a placement also supported by osteological characters.",,,,,,,,M,"Muscisaxicola fluviatilis [Sclater, PL & Salvin, O, 1866]",,"A revised classification of the Xolmiini (Aves: Tyrannidae: Fluvicolinae), including a new genus for Muscisaxicola fluviatilis",https://www.mgharvey.org/docs/Chesseretal2020.pdf,Syrtidicola
14189,species,Passeriformes,Tyrannidae,Tyrant Flycatchers and Allies,Syrtidicola fluviatilis,"(Sclater, PL; Salvin, O, 1866)","Proceedings of the Zoological Society of London 1866, pt.2 p.187",Little Ground Tyrant,Little Ground-Tyrant,Little Ground-tyrant,,,"southeastern Colombia to eastern Ecuador, eastern Peru, northern Bolivia, and southwestern Amazonian Brazil",,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22700137,ligtyr1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/ligtyr1/,avibase-61F27E71,,,"lower Rio Ucayali, Peru.","Catalogue of Birds collected by Mr. E. Bartlett on the River Ucayali, Eastern Peru, with Notes and Descriptions of New Species.",https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/90954#page/245/mode/1up,Muscisaxicola fluviatilis
14190,genus,Passeriformes,Tyrannidae,Tyrant Flycatchers and Allies,Muscisaxicola,"d'Orbigny, ACVMD; de Lafresnaye, NFAA, 1837",Magasin de Zoologie 7 cl.2 p.65,,,,471,"Genus-level changes in the fluvicoline tyrant flycatchers are based on a combination of genetic (summarised by Chesser et al. 2020b; Ohlson et al. 2020), phenotypic, and behavioral data. The classification recognizes: Syrtidicola fluviatilis based on deep genetic divergence from Muscisaxicola (Chesser et al. 2020b), as well as morphological and ecological differences; Cnemarchus rufipennis based on common themes in plumage, vocalizations and ecology with C. erythropygius; Pyrope pyrope based on deep genetic divergence within the Xolmis sensu lato clade (Chesser et al. 2020b; Ohlson et al. 2020); monospecific Nengetus (N. cinereus) based on deep genetic divergence, and its distinctive plumage and ecology; Neoxolmis restricted to four species (N. rufiventris, N. rubetra, N. salinarum, and N. coronatus) which form a cohesive group based on plumage and ecology to the exclusion of cinereus; Xolmis restricted to X. irupero and X. velatus based on genetics; and Heteroxolmis dominicanus, as genetic data show it to be outside the Xolmiini and sister to Alectrurus (Chesser et al. 2020b; Ohlson et al. 2020) a placement also supported by osteological characters.",,,,,,,,M,"Muscisaxicola rufivertex [d'Orbigny & Lafresnaye, 1837]; type by subsequent designation (Gray, GR, 1840, A List of the Genera of Birds, p. 24).  ",,"Synopsis avium ab Alcide d'Orbigny,in ejus per Americam meridionalem itinere,collectarum et ab ipso viatore necnon a de Lafresnaye in ordine redactarum",,Muscisaxicola
