14375,subspecies,Passeriformes,Thamnophilidae,"Antbirds, Antshrikes, Antwrens, and Antvireos",Thamnistes anabatinus gularis,"Phelps, WH Sr; Phelps, WH Jr, 1956",Proceedings of the Biological Society of Washington 69 p.131,,,,828,"Taxa aequatorialis and gularis are treated as subspecies of Thamnistes anabatinus pending further research. Both taxa are sometimes placed in a polytypic T. aequatorialis, along with the vocally distinct rufescens. While vocalizations (Isler & Whitney 2017), size, apparent parapatry, and mitochondrial DNA data (Weir 2009) are suggestive of species-level divergence for aequatorialis, genetic data for gularis and rufescens are lacking; further research is needed to determine taxonomic affinities across the entire complex.",far northwestern Venezuela (Táchira) and (? adjacent northeastern Colombia),,,,rusant8,,avibase-338BE5B1,,,"Cerro El Tetéo, Burgua, Estado Táchira, Venezuela; 1250 meters","Three new birds from Cerro el Teteo, Venezuela, and extensions of ranges to Venezuela and Colombia.",https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/107489#page/355/mode/1up,Thamnistes anabatinus gularis
14376,subspecies,Passeriformes,Thamnophilidae,"Antbirds, Antshrikes, Antwrens, and Antvireos",Thamnistes anabatinus aequatorialis,"Sclater, PL, 1862","Proceedings of the Zoological Society of London 1861, pt.3 p.380",,,,828,"Taxa aequatorialis and gularis are treated as subspecies of Thamnistes anabatinus pending further research. Both taxa are sometimes placed in a polytypic T. aequatorialis, along with the vocally distinct rufescens. While vocalizations (Isler & Whitney 2017), size, apparent parapatry, and mitochondrial DNA data (Weir 2009) are suggestive of species-level divergence for aequatorialis, genetic data for gularis and rufescens are lacking; further research is needed to determine taxonomic affinities across the entire complex.","foothills of southeastern Colombia, eastern Ecuador, and far northern Peru",,,,rusant9,,avibase-663577EC,,,"Nanegal and Rio Napo = Rio Napo, Ecuador.","Descriptions of Twelve New Species of American Birds, of the Families Dendrocolaptidae, Formicariidae, and Tyrannidae.",https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/91152#page/476/mode/1up,Thamnistes aequatorialis
