17493,species,Passeriformes,Cinclosomatidae,Jewel-babblers and Quail-thrushes,Cinclosoma castanotum,"Gould, J, 1840","The Annals and Magazine of Natural History including Zoology, Botany, and Geology ser.1, vol.5, no.29 p.117",Chestnut Quail-thrush,Chestnut Quail-thrush,,880,"Taxon clarum (including fordianum and morgani) is treated as a species separate from Cinclosoma castanotum based on genetic evidence from mitochondrial DNA (Toon et al. 2012; Dolman & Joseph 2015), multilocus data (Dolman & Joseph 2016), and genomic analyses (McElroy et al. 2020). Although plumages show a leapfrog pattern, the sharp transition between clarum and castanotum in eastern Australia is consistent with the recognition of two species.","southeastern Australia (Yorke Peninsula, southern Flinders Ranges, eastern Mount Lofty Range, northwestern Victoria, and southwestern New South Wales, central New South Wales)",,NE,,chequt1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/chequt1/,avibase-602C7CA7,,,"Australia; type from the Belts of the Murray, South Australia, fide Gould, 1841, Proc. Zool. Soc. London, pt. 8 (1840), p. 113.",XV. Information respecting Botanical and Zoological Travellers.,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/331684#page/139/mode/1up,[Cinclosoma] castanotus
17494,species,Passeriformes,Cinclosomatidae,Jewel-babblers and Quail-thrushes,Cinclosoma clarum,"Morgan, AM, 1926",South Australian Ornithologist 8 (1925-1926) p.138,Copperback Quail-thrush,Copperback Quail-thrush,,880,"Taxon clarum (including fordianum and morgani) is treated as a species separate from Cinclosoma castanotum based on genetic evidence from mitochondrial DNA (Toon et al. 2012; Dolman & Joseph 2015), multilocus data (Dolman & Joseph 2016), and genomic analyses (McElroy et al. 2020). Although plumages show a leapfrog pattern, the sharp transition between clarum and castanotum in eastern Australia is consistent with the recognition of two species.",,,NE,,copqut1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/copqut1/,avibase-CF9BA9D6,,,"Wipipippee, ca. five miles east of the southern end of Lake Gairdner, South Australia.",Cinclosoma castanotum clarum (Chestnut-backed Ground-bird). A new subspecies.,https://birdssa.asn.au/images/saopdfs/Volume8/1926V08P138.pdf,Cinclosoma castanotum clarum
