26802,species,Passeriformes,Turdidae,Thrushes and Allies,Turdus mupinensis,"Laubmann, AL, 1920",Ornithologische Monatsberichte 28 p.17,Chinese Thrush,Chinese Thrush,Chinese Thrush,91,"Taxon mupinensis (monotypic) is placed in the genus Turdus based on available evidence. Sometimes placed in the monospecific genus Otocichla, mitochondrial-dominated DNA data do not provide unambiguous support for this arrangement (Nylander et al. 2008; Batista et al. 2020), and genomic data for mupinensis is lacking (Batista et al. 2020); further research desirable.","western China (southern Gansu to Shaanxi, Hubei, Sichuan, and northwestern Yunnan)",,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22708826,chithr2,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/chithr2/,avibase-4435BAA4,,,,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/101390#page/27/mode/1up,Turdus mupinensis
26803,species,Passeriformes,Turdidae,Thrushes and Allies,Turdus simensis,"(Rüppell, WPES, 1837)","Neue Wirbelthiere zu der Fauna von Abyssinien gehörig. Vögel p.81, pl. 29, fig. 1",Ethiopian Thrush,Ethiopian Thrush,Ethiopian Thrush,"92, 93","92)) Taxa litsitsirupa (polytypic) and simensis (monotypic) are placed in the genus Turdus based on available evidence. Sometimes placed in the monospecific genus Psophocichla, genomic data recover them as part of a basal polytomy within an expanded Turdus (Batista et al. 2020) and this treatment is adopted pending further research. 93)) Taxon simensis is treated as a species separate from Turdus litsitsirupa based on moderate divergence in mitochondrial DNA (Nylander et al. 2008), and apparent differences in vocalizations; further genetic research and formal bioacoustic analyses desirable.",highlands of Eritrea and Ethiopia,,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/103887388,grothr2,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/grothr2/,avibase-E8E2ED6D,,,"Angetkat, northern Abyssinia.",,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/114234#page/161/mode/1up,Merula (Turdus) simensis
26804,species,Passeriformes,Turdidae,Thrushes and Allies,Turdus litsitsirupa,"(Smith, A, 1836)",Report of the Expedition for Exploring Central Africa from the Cape of Good Hope (etc.) p.45,Groundscraper Thrush,Groundscraper Thrush,Groundscraper Thrush,"92, 93","92)) Taxa litsitsirupa (polytypic) and simensis (monotypic) are placed in the genus Turdus based on available evidence. Sometimes placed in the monospecific genus Psophocichla, genomic data recover them as part of a basal polytomy within an expanded Turdus (Batista et al. 2020) and this treatment is adopted pending further research. 93)) Taxon simensis is treated as a species separate from Turdus litsitsirupa based on moderate divergence in mitochondrial DNA (Nylander et al. 2008), and apparent differences in vocalizations; further genetic research and formal bioacoustic analyses desirable.",,,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/103887337,grothr1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/grothr1/,avibase-8C5759C5,,,between the Orange River and the Tropic.,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/64243#page/125/mode/1up,Merula litsitsirupa
