32814,genus,Passeriformes,Thraupidae,Tanagers and Allies,Acanthidops,"Ridgway, R, 1882",Proceedings of the United States National Museum 4 p.335,,,,925,"Taxon bairdi is placed in the monotypic genus Acanthidops pending further research. Mitochondrial-dominated DNA data (Burns et al. 2014) suggest that A. bairdi is embedded in Haplospiza and this clade is sister to Geospizopsis. While merging Acanthidops into Haplospiza is plausible, support for the position of H. unicolor is low, and individual gene trees produced conflicting results. Furthermore, Barker et al. (2015), using the same data, recovered a different topology for these five species. Additional data is needed to better resolve this phylogeny.",,,,,,,,M,"Acanthidops bairdii [Ridgway, 1882]; type by original designation",,,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/53433#page/353/mode/1up,Acanthidops
32815,species,Passeriformes,Thraupidae,Tanagers and Allies,Acanthidops bairdi,"Ridgway, R, 1882","Proceedings of the United States National Museum 4, no.235 p.336",Peg-billed Finch,Peg-billed Finch,Peg-billed Finch,925,"Taxon bairdi is placed in the monotypic genus Acanthidops pending further research. Mitochondrial-dominated DNA data (Burns et al. 2014) suggest that A. bairdi is embedded in Haplospiza and this clade is sister to Geospizopsis. While merging Acanthidops into Haplospiza is plausible, support for the position of H. unicolor is low, and individual gene trees produced conflicting results. Furthermore, Barker et al. (2015), using the same data, recovered a different topology for these five species. Additional data is needed to better resolve this phylogeny.",high volcanic peaks of Costa Rica and western Panama,,LC,https://datazone.birdlife.org/species/factsheet/22723128,pebfin1,https://birdsoftheworld.org/bow/species/pebfin1/,avibase-5CCF7540,,,"Volcan de Irazu, Costa Rica.",,https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/53433#page/354/mode/1up,Acanthidops bairdi
